regex_search2 [SimTalk]
Searches for the specified Text in the passed text that matches the RegularExpression.
Remarks
The function regex_search2 corresponds to the function regex_search. As opposed to it the return value
of regex_search2 is an array of data type string containing all found partial expressions, followed by the
preceding text, followed by the text at the end.
Note
To search for a backslash (\) and replace it with another character, type in four backslashes, \\\\.
This is because the SimTalk parser uses the backslash as an escape character.
// replaces the backslash with an empty space
regex_replace("my\\text", "\\\\", " ")
print regex_replace("my\\text", "\\\\", " ")
-- outputs my text
Type
Function
Syntax
regex_search2(Text:string, RegularExpression:string) → string[]
Parameters
You can specify the following parameters:
• The parameter Text of data type string designates the text which you want to search for the regular
expression.
• The parameter RegularExpression of data type string designates the regular expression that you want to
find.
Return Value
The return value is an array of data type string.
regex_search2 [SimTalk]
12-328 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Examples
print regex_search2("John.Doe@acme.com", "(\w*)\.(\w*)@(\w*)\.(\w*)")
[John.Doe@acme.com, John, Doe, acme, com, , ]
print regex_search2("EMail:John.Doe@acme.com [John Doe]", ":(\w*)\.
(\w*)@(\w*)\.(\w*)")
[:John.Doe@acme.com, John, Doe, acme, com, EMail,  [John Doe]]
See also
regex_replace [SimTalk]
regex_search [SimTalk]
regex_search2 [SimTalk]
Plant Simulation Help 12-329
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

splitString [SimTalk]
Splits a string into its individual string components.
Type
Function
Syntax
splitString(Text:string, Delimiter:string[, KeepEmptyItems:boolean:=false]) 
→ string[]
Parameters
You can specify the following parameters:
• The parameter Text of data type string designates the string which you want to split.
• The parameter Delimiter of data type string designates the delimiter that was used in the parameter Text
to separate its components.
Note
The delimiter can be any character.
If the parameter Delimiter is empty, the result/return value is an array of the individual letters of the text
that is to be split up.
• The optional parameter KeepEmptyItems of data type boolean sets if empty strings are to be
incorporated into the array (true) or not (false).
Default Value of the Parameter
The default value is false.
Return Value
The return value is an array of data type string.
splitString [SimTalk]
12-330 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Examples
print splitString("a,b,c,d", ",")       -- returns [a, b, c, d] in the 
Console
print splitString("one,two;three;four,five", ",;") 
-- returns [one, two, three, four, five] in the Console
print splitString("a;b;;c", ";", true) -- returns [a, b, , c] in the Console
print splitString("a;b;;c", ";")       -- returns [a, b, c]
var text:string := "Hello World"
var letters:string[] := splitString(text, "")
print letters
-- returns [H, e, l, l, o,  , W, o, r, l, d]
splitString [SimTalk]
Plant Simulation Help 12-331
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

splitStringToNum [SimTalk]
Splits a string into an array of numbers of data type real.
Type
Function
Syntax
splitStringToNum(Text:string, Delimiter:string) → real
Parameters
You can specify the following parameters:
• The parameter Text of data type string designates the real numbers which you want to split.
• The parameter Delimiter of data type string designates the delimiter that was used in the parameter Text
to separate its components.
Return Value
The return value is an array of data type real.
Example
print splitStringToNum("1.2;2.8!3.4;4.5;6.8!3.6", ";!") 
// returns [1.2, 2.8, 3.4, 4.5, 6.8, 3.6] in the Console
